---
title: "Calculated Columns - Dialog"
description: "Configure the dialog that $framework Data Grid users create and edit calculated columns in: opening it, column references, the expression editor, data types, when edits apply, and customising the columns users create."
enterprise: true
---

Users add and edit [Calculated Columns](./calculated-columns/) in the Calculated Column dialog, which has three fields: **Title**, **Type** and **Expression**.

Configure the dialog with the `calculatedColumns` grid option. Set `calculatedColumns: true` to enable the feature with default dialog options, or provide an options object to enable the feature and customise the dialog.

## Opening the Dialog

Users open the dialog from the grid's menus; see [Letting Users Add Columns](./calculated-columns/#letting-users-add-columns). To open it from your own interface, call one of these grid API methods:

- `openCalculatedColumnDialog(column)` to edit or view an existing calculated column, bringing its dialog forward if already open.
- `openAddCalculatedColumnDialog(sourceColumn?)` to add one after a source column, or at the end when omitted.

Both methods focus the dialog and use the configured [apply mode](#applymode). Adding is unavailable in pivot mode.

```javascript
// Call from an application button.
function onAddCalculatedColumn() {
    gridApi.openAddCalculatedColumnDialog();
}
```

Closing the dialog returns focus to the calculated column's header, scrolling it into view if needed. If a deferred Add was not applied, focus returns to the source column when supplied. Focus is not moved if you have already focused another element.

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-api/api.json" section="accessories" names=["openCalculatedColumnDialog", "openAddCalculatedColumnDialog"] /%}

## Referencing Columns {% #references-with-column-groups %}

The dialog shows references by header name, for example `[Revenue]`. It translates these display references back to `colId` references before updating the column definition. Some headers need more than the header name:

- **Duplicate headers under column groups:** the dialog uses the shortest unique group path, such as `[2025 Q4]` and `[2026 Q4]`.
- **Same header _and_ same group hierarchy:** the dialog falls back to appending the `colId`, for example `[2025 Q4 (revenue-q4)]`. This keeps the reference stable when columns are reordered. Give your columns distinct headers or group hierarchies to avoid this.
- **A `]` in the header:** a literal `]` in a header name is escaped as `]]`. For example, the header `Revenue [USD]` appears as `[Revenue [USD]]]`. The Columns picker inserts the escaped reference automatically; stored `colId` references are unchanged.

The following example has duplicate `Q1`, `Q2`, `Q3` and `Q4` headers under `2025` and `2026` column groups. The year groups start collapsed and show a calculated `Total` column; expanding a group reveals the quarter columns. The dialog shows grouped references such as `[2025 Q4]` and `[2026 Q4]`, while the source code stores stable `colId` references such as `[q4_2025]` and `[q4_2026]`.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Column Groups with Duplicate Headers" name="calculated-columns-column-groups" /%}

## Expression Editor

The expression editor highlights references, functions and operators. Built-in errors mark the field invalid and have underlines, tooltips and accessible descriptions. Reference checks validate columns, not row availability.

In live mode, invalid expressions still apply; empty expressions remain valid. [Custom validation and reference restrictions](./calculated-columns-validation/) still apply. In deferred edit dialogs, validation runs as you edit and on **Apply**, not when the dialog opens.

### Expression Pickers {% #expressionpickers %}

Buttons beside the expression open the **Columns**, **Functions** and **Operators** pickers. Use `expressionPickers` to control which expression pickers appear; by default all three do:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    calculatedColumns: {
        expressionPickers: ['columns'],
    },
};
```

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="CalculatedColumnExpressionPicker" config={"asCode":true} /%}

This only controls the expression picker buttons. Use an empty array or `null` to hide all picker buttons. Inline autocomplete while typing in the expression editor remains available.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Dialog Expression Pickers" name="calculated-columns-dialog-helper-lists" /%}

In the example, edit **Profit** to see that only the **Columns** picker is shown.

## Data Types {% #datatypes %}

Use `dataTypes` to control which cell data types appear in the dialog's **Type** dropdown. The default is `['text', 'number', 'date', 'boolean']`. The list can include built-in cell data types and custom types registered with `dataTypeDefinitions`:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    dataTypeDefinitions: {
        currency: {
            extendsDataType: 'number',
            baseDataType: 'number',
            valueFormatter: params =>
                params.value == null ? '' : `$${params.value.toLocaleString()}`,
        },
    },
    calculatedColumns: {
        dataTypes: ['currency', 'number', 'boolean'],
    },
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'revenue', cellDataType: 'currency' },
        { field: 'cost', cellDataType: 'currency' },
        {
            colId: 'profit',
            headerName: 'Profit',
            calculatedExpression: '[revenue] - [cost]',
            cellDataType: 'currency',
        },
    ],
};
```

- Applying the type to your own columns as well means a user who picks **Currency** in the dialog gets the same formatting as the columns you declared.
- A `valueFormatter` on a column definition cannot be selected in the dialog, so it is not available to users.
- Built-in types use the grid's locale text, while custom type names are converted to readable labels in the dialog.
- If a selected value does not match a registered [Cell Data Type](./cell-data-types/), the grid's normal `cellDataType` validation applies when the calculated column is created.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Dialog Data Types" name="calculated-columns-dialog-data-types" /%}

In the example, edit **Profit** and open the **Type** dropdown to see **Currency**, **Number** and **Boolean**.

## Applying Changes {% #applymode %}

The `applyMode` option sets when dialog edits reach the column. The default is `'live'`:

|                            | Live (default)                                                                                                                       | Deferred (`applyMode: 'deferred'`)                                                          |
| -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Title, type and expression | Update the column as you type                                                                                                        | Held until the user clicks **Apply**; the dialog shows **Apply** and **Cancel** buttons     |
| **Add Calculated Column**  | Creates the column and opens the dialog over it                                                                                      | Creates the column when the user clicks **Apply**                                           |
| Invalid expressions        | Still apply: invalid or incomplete expressions render formula errors until fixed. Restricted references are the exception: see below | Validated before applying                                                                   |
| Rejected edits             | See [How Rejected Edits Behave](./calculated-columns-validation/#how-rejected-edits-behave)                                          | See [How Rejected Edits Behave](./calculated-columns-validation/#how-rejected-edits-behave) |

In live mode:

- Closing the dialog keeps the latest state; remove the column with **Remove Calculated Column**.
- An empty expression renders blank cells.
- Expression edits containing references excluded by [`isColumnReferenceable`](./calculated-columns-validation/#iscolumnreferenceable) are saved but blocked from evaluation, preserving the full text for correction.

### Deferred Mode

Set `applyMode: 'deferred'` to hold changes until you click **Apply** instead. The dialog shows Apply and Cancel buttons and validates edits before applying them:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    calculatedColumns: {
        applyMode: 'deferred',
    },
};
```

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="CalculatedColumnApplyMode" config={"asCode":true} /%}

{% gridExampleRunner title="Deferred Apply Mode" name="calculated-columns-apply-mode" /%}

In the example, edit **Profit** and change its expression: the cells change only when you click **Apply**.

### Debouncing Live Edits {% #applydebounce %}

Set `applyDebounce` to wait for a quiet period before applying live dialog edits. This delays both the cell repaint and calculated-column change events:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    calculatedColumns: {
        applyDebounce: 400,
    },
};
```

- The default is `0`, which applies on the next animation frame.
- Closing the dialog applies any pending edit immediately; removing the column cancels it.
- State and column-definition APIs report the last applied values while an edit is pending.
- This option has no effect in deferred mode.

## Column Highlighting {% #suppresscolumnhighlighting %}

While a column's dialog is open, the grid highlights the column's header and cells. Customise the highlight colour with the `--ag-calculated-column-highlight-color` CSS variable.

Use `suppressColumnHighlighting` to disable the highlight while the dialog is open. The default is `false`:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    calculatedColumns: {
        suppressColumnHighlighting: true,
    },
};
```

{% gridExampleRunner title="Suppress Dialog Column Highlighting" name="calculated-columns-dialog-highlighting" /%}

In the example, edit **Profit**: the column is not highlighted while its dialog is open.

## Customising User-Created Columns {% #processcoldef %}

Use `processColDef` to customise calculated columns that users create in the dialog, for example to add a filter or allow aggregation. It receives the column's own definition, as set in the dialog, and returns the definition to use. The returned definition takes precedence over `defaultColDef` and column types:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    calculatedColumns: {
        processColDef: (params) => ({
            ...params.colDef,
            filter: true,
            enableValue: params.colDef.cellDataType === 'number',
        }),
    },
};
```

Spread `params.colDef` to preserve the user's settings. Omitted properties use normal column defaults, except `calculatedExpression`, which retains the user's expression.

In the example, the **Profit** column is restored from Grid State as if a user had created it, so it has a filter button and can be aggregated from the column menu.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Customising Calculated Column Definitions" name="calculated-columns-process-col-def" /%}

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="CalculatedColumnProcessColDefParams" /%}

`params.expressionValidation` includes syntax and reference errors plus saved dialog rejections from `getValidationErrors` and `isColumnReferenceable`, matching `expressionValidation` on the [calculatedColumnChanged](./calculated-columns-api/#events) event. It does not rerun these callbacks or check row-specific evaluation errors.

### When the Callback Runs

- The callback runs each time the column is built, including when dialog edits are applied and on restore from [Grid State](./grid-state/).
- It does not run for calculated columns declared in `columnDefs`.
- The dialog and Grid State keep the user's values, not the callback's.
- `colId`, `editable`, `suppressPaste`, `field`, `valueGetter`, `valueSetter`, `cellEditor` and `cellEditorSelector` are always overridden.
- Prefer `initial*` properties, such as `initialWidth`, to stateful ones.
- The definitions from `api.getColumnDefs()` include the callback's output; see [Managing Columns with Column Definitions](./calculated-columns-api/#managing-columns-with-column-definitions).

### Making Created Columns Read-Only

`processColDef` can return `calculatedColumnReadOnly: true` to make user-created columns read-only. In live mode, `calculatedColumnReadOnly: true` locks the column when the initial Add dialog closes, even if the expression is empty or invalid.

To leave blank columns editable, return the full definition with the flag `true` only when there is an expression: `{ ...params.colDef, calculatedColumnReadOnly: !!params.colDef.calculatedExpression?.trim() }`.

A non-empty expression may still be unfinished; use `applyMode: 'deferred'` to require Apply before creating and locking the column.

See [Read-Only Calculated Columns](./calculated-columns-validation/#read-only-calculated-columns) for what a read-only column allows.
